e of ~10/100/10^6 values — after 3 decimals, the chance of exact half boundary is tiny but over 10^6 points and many computations... The C program computes distance sum with sqrt accumulating, then divides. std::format vs printf vs .NET rounding differences could produce 1-last-digit differences on some runs. Byte comparison would fail spuriously.\n\nThe robust approach for floating comparison (per §6.1: \"Сравнение вещественных в тестах: ... либо с допуском в exe-тесте\"). So the recommended approach: a small exe that parses both outputs and compares numbers with tolerance. But that's more infra. Alternatively: since output is formatted to 3 decimals, comparing formatted strings is the \"фиксированный формат вывода (3 знака — тогда fc честен)\" — the section says with 3 decimals fc is honest IF the formatting rounds identically. But rounding mode differences between libs could break it. However — the program and reference both produce the same decimal values from the same input; if formatting is identical, fc works. The concern is only rounding-mode at the boundary. std::format and printf in MSVC both use the same CRT underlying (MSVC's printf and std::format both round-half-even? .NET F3 also round-half-even). Actually all three round half-to-even per IEEE 754 default rounding. std::format uses the same rounding as printf? std::format implements its own but rounds half-to-even per spec (chars_format). PowerShell .NET rounds half-to-even. MSVC printf — I recall MSVC CRT printf rounds half away from zero for exact ties, which DIFFERS from std::format and .NET! Actually no — MSVC's printf historically used round-half-away-from-zero? There was a known issue where MSVC's printf didn't round correctly and was fixed... Honestly, uncertain. This is exactly the kind of thing to flag: [ФАКТ/НЕ УВЕРЕНА — проверить].\n\nGiven uncertainty, the plan should include a verification step: generate the same input, run all three, diff; if a mismatch appears at a tie, handle by tolerance-based comparison or by comparing formatted strings and accepting the risk documented.\n\nHmm, but I shouldn't overload. Let me structure the plan and ask 2-3 clarifying questions:\n\nQuestions for the student:\n1. Etalon for mass tests: run t3_bbox_ref.ps1 on the fly (matches §6.1) vs committed expected files (t2 pattern)? Recommend on-the-fly per the task statement.\n2. gen_cloud exe location: put in $(BINDIR)\\tools (reuse) — probably fine, minor.\n3. Comparison method: byte/fc vs tolerance exe. Given §6.1 allows \"3 знака — тогда fc честен\", and to keep infra minimal (no new tool), use fc on formatted output; but flag rounding-mode risk and offer tolerance-exe option.\n\nAlso: the `test` phony currently depends on `format-check`. For t3 we follow same.\n\nAlso need to add `t3_bbox_c` to C_DIRS, T3_C_EXE COMMON_FLAG, link rule via common_c.dll/lib, and add T3 tests to `test` and T3_C_EXE to `t3` phony and to `all`.\n\nLet me also double-check: T3_CPP_OBJ commented at line 66 but not needed since rule uses main.obj + dll. For T3_C we'll mirror: `$(BINDIR)\\t3_bbox_c\\main.exe: $(BINDIR)\\t3_bbox_c\\main.obj $(BINDIR)\\common\\common_c.dll | $(BINDIR)\\t3_bbox_c` with set PATH and link common_c.lib.\n\nNote the link rule uses `set \"PATH=%~dp0\\$(BINDIR)\\common;%PATH%\"` then link. But in make, recipes are separate shell invocations; `set` affects only that one cmd invocation, which is fine since it's the same line as link (joined by newline in one recipe → one cmd session). Actually the recipe has two lines: `set ...` and `$(LINK) ...` — both run in the same cmd.exe instance (make executes the whole recipe via one shell). OK.\n\nAlso the tools rules: check_contains has a typo in obj name (`check_contain.obj` vs `check_contains.obj`) — cosmetic, /Fo writes that obj. Not critical.\n\nAlso gen_cloud rule needs encoding? gen_cloud outputs numbers only — no encoding issue.\n\nOne more: data generation nodes (§5 p15: \"Данные тестов — тоже узлы графа\"). So:\n- `$(