          "text": "The manual explains the concept but doesn't explicitly say \"the recipe runs once per out-of-date target\". Let me recall the precise behavior. Actually in GNU make, for independent targets, each target is treated as its own rule. When make processes a rule with multiple independent targets, it executes the recipe once for each target that needs updating. The key insight for Irina's question: make decides which targets need updating **before** running any recipe. But actually, the process is: make processes goals in order. When it tries to build a target, it checks if it's out of date. For missing targets, it considers them out of date and runs the recipe.\n\nThe subtle point: make runs the recipe for the first missing target (say `foo`), which also creates `bar`. Then when make gets to `bar`, it checks whether `bar` needs updating. `bar` now exists... but wait — the decision about whether `bar` needs updating was already made? Or is it re-evaluated?\n\nActually the manual's own example (bigoutput/littleoutput) shows the recipe typically uses `$@` to make different output. If both are missing, make runs the recipe twice, once for each. In the dll+lib case, the recipe creates BOTH files regardless of $@, so running twice is redundant.\n\nThe reason make runs the recipe twice when both are missing: make evaluates each target independently. When it processes the goal list, it determines for each goal whether it is out of date. `foo` is missing → out of date → needs recipe. `bar` is missing → out of date → needs recipe. It then runs the recipe for `foo`, creating both foo and bar. Then for `bar`, it runs the recipe again.\n\nWait, but would make re-check whether bar is still out of date after foo's recipe ran? In GNU make's update_goal_chain, goals are processed one at a time, and after remaking a goal it proceeds to the next. When it gets to `bar`, it checks the file again. Since `bar` now exists and is newer than prerequisites... hmm, actually it should notice bar exists now.\n\nLet me think more carefully. Actually the behavior: In GNU make, when you have `foo bar: baz`, and both are missing, make processes the rule. The way it works internally: the rule with multiple targets is expanded into multiple rules. When make tries to update `foo`, it runs the recipe. The recipe creates foo (and bar). Then when make processes `bar` as a goal (because it was in the command line or is a prerequisite), it re-checks `bar`: it exists now, timestamp newer than baz → not out of date → recipe not run.\n\nHmm, but that would contradict my earlier claim. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, I recall there's nuance: if both targets are given as goals on the command line (e.g., `make foo bar`), make processes them in sequence, and after remaking foo it will see bar exists. But there's a known behavior documented elsewhere: \"the recipe is run once for each target that needs updating\". The manual section here doesn't state it explicitly, but I remember from the GNU make manual in the section on grouped targets introduction (make 4.3 release notes):\n\nFrom the make 4.3 NEWS:\n\"New feature: Grouped targets: if a rule has multiple targets, and the recipe is run once for all targets... previously the recipe was run once per target.\"\n\nActually let me recall the actual NEWS. GNU make 4.3 NEWS:\n\"* New feature: The '&:' syntax for grouped targets. ... Previously, a rule with multiple targets would run the recipe once for each target.\"\n\nYes, that's the key. Before grouped targets, a rule with multiple targets ran the recipe once per target. With `&:`, it runs once for all.\n\nSo for Irina's question: why does make run the recipe for the second target if lib already exists?\n\nThe answer: because make decides whether a target needs updating based on the state of the filesystem at the time of decision. When both dll and lib are missing, make has already decided both need updating. The way GNU make processes independent targets: it doesn't re-evaluate the second target after the first recipe ran. The set of targets needing update is determined up front (or, more precisely, each target is processed and marked as needing the recipe; the recipe is invoked once per target that needed updating).\n\nHmm, but actually I need to be careful about the mechanism. Let me think about what actually happens in GNU make with `make foo bar` where rule is `foo bar: baz` and recipe creates both.\n\nI believe the actual behavior: make determines for each goal whether it's out of date. For `foo` (missing) → needs update → run recipe → creates foo, bar. For `bar` → now make checks: does bar exist? Yes. Is it out of date relative to baz? No (freshly created). So... it would NOT run the recipe again.\n\nBut that contradicts the documented \"run once per target\". Hmm.\n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider. The documented behavior in the manual (4.10 and NEWS) is that the recipe runs once per target that needs updating. Let me search my memory: GNU make 4.3 NEWS says:\n\n\"Previously, when a rule had multiple targets, the recipe would be run once for each target that needed to be updated. Now you can use '&:' to make the recipe run only once if any of the targets needs updating.\"\n\nActually the precise wording from NEWS 4.3:\n\"* New feature: Grouped Targets: it is now possible to specify that the recipe of a rule updates a group of targets. ... Rather than running the recipe once for each target in the group, it will be run only once, and all the targets in the group will be considered updated.\"\n\nSo this confirms: with `:` multi-target, recipe runs once per target. The mechanism: when make updates goals, for each target it checks update status and runs recipe. The key question is whether the second target's recipe is skipped because the file now exists.\n\nI recall the specific implementation detail: In GNU make, `update_file` is called for each target. When a rule has multiple targets, they are all added to the \"goal chain\" or processed. Each target that is out-of-date causes the recipe to run. If the first run creates the second file, then when the second target is processed... \n\nHmm, I genuinely am not 100% sure about the re-evaluation. Let me think about the actual test case people complain about. The classic complaint: `a b: c\\n\\t touch a; touch b` — people say make runs it twice. Actually yes, this is a known gotcha: if both a and b are missing, `make a b` runs the recipe twice. I'm fairly confident this is the real behavior.\n\nThe reason: make determines whether each target is out of date when it encounters it in the update chain. The update_goal_chain processes goals. But actually the reason it runs twice: make checks each target's timestamp BEFORE running any recipe. Or more precisely: the decision of \"needs updating\" for `bar` was made before `foo`'s recipe ran.\n\nActually, I found the real mechanism from the GNU make source / docs: For independent targets, make processes each target independently. When both are missing, both are \"out of date\". Make runs the recipe for the first target. Then for the second target, make checks: is it still out of date? In GNU make, the timestamp caching... Actually the known behavior (and the reason grouped targets were introduced) is definitely that it runs once per target when both need updating.\n\nI should be honest about the mechanism: the safest factual statement is the documented one — the recipe runs once for each target that needs updating; when both are missing/out-of-date, it runs twice. And explain why conceptually: make treats them as independent rules; for each target it independently decides \"needs rebuild\" and runs the recipe. The recipe for `foo` is assumed to rebuild `foo`; make doesn't know it also creates `bar`. Actually, I realize I should verify the exact re-check behavior rather than assert.\n\nLet me not over-assert on the fine-grained mechanism. The core answer to Irina's question:\n\nHer intuition: \"if the recipe for dll runs first and creates lib, why run the recipe for lib a second time if lib already exists?\"\n\nThe resolution: make decides whether a target needs updating when it processes that target, using the state of the filesystem at that decision point. With independent targets (`:`), make has already decided (before running any recipe) which targets need updating — and both dll and lib are missing, so both are marked. The recipe is then invoked once per marked target. make does not \"notice\" that the first invocation created the second file, because its model is: each target is a separate goal with its own recipe invocation. It doesn't infer side effects.\n\nHmm, but the re-check... I want to give an accurate answer. Let me give the answer at the right level of precision:\n\nThe accurate model per GNU make: a multi-target rule with `:` is exactly equivalent to separate rules. Each target is remade independently: make checks \"does target need updating?\" — if yes, run recipe. The recipe is run for each target that needs updating. When both files are missing, both \"need updating\" → recipe runs twice. The fact that the first run created lib doesn't prevent the second run, because the second target's \"needs updating\" decision was already made and make doesn't re-derive it from the recipe's side effects (make has no idea the recipe creates both files).\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether make re-checks. I'll look at the actual behavior description. In GNU make manual 4.10: \"Rules with independent targets ... is equivalent to writing the same rule once for each target\". So `make a b` where rule `a b: c` with recipe → equivalent to two separate rules, so make builds a (recipe), then builds b — but now b exists, so does it rebuild? For a separate rule `b: c` with recipe, make would check: b exists and is newer than c → not out of date → skip. So by strict \"equivalent to separate rules\" logic, it would NOT run twice if the first recipe created b.\n\nBut the manual NEWS for grouped targets says the OLD behavior was \"run once per target\". So there's a tension. The resolution: the equivalence is about the rule structure, but when make is asked to update multiple goals and the recipe creates both... \n\nOK here's the actual truth from GNU make behavior (I'm now recalling concrete evidence): The classic gotcha IS real — people hit \"recipe runs twice\" with multi-target. The NEWS statement is authoritative: old behavior ran recipe once per target needing update.\n\nWhy does it run twice even though b exists after first run? Because make computes the set of out-of-date targets during the update phase, and for independent targets, each target in the rule is considered separately: when make updates the file `a`, it runs the recipe. When it updates file `b`, b is now fresh... \n\nI think the real reason is subtle and I shouldn't over-claim the exact internal mechanism. Let me verify with an actual source or a more authoritative doc. Actually, I could test it! I'm on Windows with make available. But plan mode forbids modifications... running a test that creates temp files is \"modifying the system\" (creating files in temp dir). Hmm, the temp dir is pre-approved for work, but plan mode says read-only, no changes. Creating temp test files would violate that. I should not run it.\n\nBut I can webfetch the make NEWS to get the authoritative statement. Let me do that to be accurate.\n\nActually, let me just fetch the NEWS.",
