        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Now I have authoritative sources. Let me answer Irina's question precisely.\n\nHer question: \"выполнение дважды: не совсем понимаю. например рецепт для dll выполнится первым, возникнет и lib; Так зачем выполнять цель для lib второй раз если lib уже есть?\"\n\nSo she's asking: why would the recipe run twice, if after the first run the .lib already exists?\n\nKey point to explain: In GNU make, when you write `a b: dep` (independent targets), it's exactly like two rules. When make builds a goal, it considers each target separately. For the \"dll lib: obj\" rule, make would run the recipe for each target that needs updating. But the critical question is the ORDER of decision-making.\n\nActually, the precise behavior: make decides for each target independently whether it needs updating, based on timestamps. When both dll and lib are missing, both are \"out of date\". make will run the recipe for the first target (creating both files), then... will it run again for the second?\n\nFrom the manual: \"This is equivalent to writing the same rule once for each target, with duplicated prerequisites and recipes.\"\n\nSo it's as if:\n```\ndll: obj\n\tlink ...\nlib: obj\n\tlink ...\n```\n\nNow, when make builds `lib`, it checks: does lib need updating? lib now exists (created as side-effect of dll's recipe) and is newer than obj → so it does NOT need updating → recipe NOT run.\n\nHmm wait, that contradicts what I said earlier. Let me think carefully.\n\nActually, the NEWS for 4.4 says: \"Previously each target in a explicit grouped target rule was considered individually: if the targets needed by the build were not out of date the recipe was not run even if other targets in the group were out of date.\"\n\nThat's about grouped targets (&:), not independent.\n\nFor independent targets, the recipe is duplicated per target. make evaluates each target's update status separately. The question is whether make's evaluation happens before or after the first recipe runs.\n\nActually, the key subtlety: make processes goals sequentially. When you ask make to build `lib` (e.g. as a prerequisite of main.exe), make checks: does lib exist? At decision time (before running any recipe), if lib doesn't exist → out of date → run its recipe.\n\nBut if the dll recipe ran first and created lib... does make re-check? \n\nThe real behavior: In GNU make, when there's a rule `dll lib: obj`, make considers both targets. If you build main.exe which depends on lib, make will look at the rule for lib. At that moment, is lib's recipe run once or twice?\n\nActually here's the important documented behavior from manual 4.10: the recipe is duplicated. But make doesn't KNOW that the dll recipe creates lib. The recipe for dll is `link /DLL /OUT:dll`. From make's perspective, that recipe \"builds dll\". It doesn't know it also creates lib.\n\nSo the scenario: clean build, main.exe depends on (prereq) lib.\n1. make needs to build lib.\n2. make looks for a rule to build lib: finds `dll lib: obj` → recipe `link /DLL...`.\n3. Before running, make checks prerequisites of lib: obj. obj needs building? If obj up to date, and lib doesn't exist → lib is out of date → run recipe.\n\nBut wait — make would run the recipe for lib, with $@ = lib. The recipe `link /DLL /OUT:...` — but this recipe uses $@ which would be lib! So the recipe would actually create a file named \"lib\" as output — wrong. That's why with independent targets you typically use $@ in the recipe.\n\nBut the real issue in our case: make might also need to build dll (if dll is a prereq of something else, or the two targets are in the same rule). \n\nActually, let me reconsider. The precise, documented behavior:\n\nFrom the manual: \"This is equivalent to writing the same rule once for each target, with duplicated prerequisites and recipes.\" — meaning:\n```\ndll: obj\n\tlink /DLL /OUT:$(BINDIR)\\common\\common_cpp.dll $^\nlib: obj\n\tlink /DLL /OUT:$(BINDIR)\\common\\common_cpp.dll $^\n```\n\nNow if main.exe requires BOTH dll and lib (both as prereqs), make must ensure both exist. Processing order:\n1. make checks dll: doesn't exist → out of date → run recipe → creates dll AND lib (side effect).\n2. make checks lib: NOW it exists and is newer than obj → up to date → skip.\n\nSo actually with both as prereqs, the recipe runs ONCE, not twice! Because after the first run, lib exists.\n\nHmm, but that contradicts what I said earlier (\"runs twice\"). Let me reconsider when it would run twice.\n\nThe recipe runs twice when... make decides both need updating. When does it decide both need updating? If make processes both goals in parallel, or if it decides on dll first then lib before dll's recipe has executed. Actually make processes sequentially by default. \n\nActually wait — the real subtlety. GNU make's behavior: when it needs to update a target, it first updates all prerequisites, then runs the recipe. The check \"does this target need updating\" uses file timestamps. \n\nThe documented caveat in the manual is about a DIFFERENT problem: the recipe might run once per target when you invoke `make dll lib` explicitly as goals, or in parallel builds. But actually let me recall the NEWS for 4.4 again: \"Previously each target in a explicit grouped target rule was considered individually...\"\n\nThat was about grouped targets being considered individually — i.e., with `&:`, before 4.4, each target was considered individually. Now if any is out of date, all considered updated.\n\nFor independent targets (classic `:`), the behavior per manual 4.10: it's equivalent to separate rules. When building, each target's \"need update\" is evaluated. The recipe runs for each target that needs updating.\n\nThe concrete scenario where double-execution happens: Suppose lib and dll are both prerequisites of main.exe (both in the prereq list). make will ensure both exist. It evaluates goals. When make starts updating main.exe, it processes prereqs in order. First prereq: dll. dll missing → out of date → recipe runs → creates dll and lib. Then next prereq: lib. lib now exists, newer than obj → not out of date → skip. So recipe runs ONCE here.\n\nBut if you run `make lib dll` explicitly (both as goals) — make processes goals sequentially: first lib → missing → run recipe (creates lib AND dll). Then goal dll → exists now → skip. So once.\n\nHmm so when does it run twice? \n\nActually — important nuance: if the recipe is run for `dll`, `$@` = dll, and the recipe explicitly names the output `/OUT:...dll`, it creates both. Fine. But if the target that was \"needed\" is lib, and recipe has hardcoded /OUT:dll, then it creates dll not lib — lib still missing! That's the actual hazard: with independent targets and a recipe that hardcodes output for only one of the targets, make may run the recipe for EACH target that needs updating, and the recipe may not actually create the requested target.\n\nBut in our dll+lib case with hardcoded /OUT:common_cpp.dll: if make decides lib needs updating and runs recipe, it creates common_cpp.dll (not \"lib\"). lib would still not exist... but the recipe creates lib as side-effect of the DLL build (link creates the import lib alongside). So lib gets created. OK.\n\nBut the double-execution concern: the well-known gotcha is precisely what the NEWS 4.4 describes. Let me look at the actual historical behavior:\n\nThe classic documentation quote (from older GNU make manual, the famous example):\n```\nbigoutput littleoutput : text.g\n        generate text.g -$(subst output,,$@) > $@\n```\n\"make will run the recipe once for each target that needs to be remade.\"\n\nKey: \"once for each target that needs to be remade\". If both bigoutput and littleoutput are missing, make runs the recipe twice — once for bigoutput, once for littleoutput. Because make considers them independent and each is missing.\n\nBUT in practice, does make run the recipe for the second target after the first created it? The recipe `generate text.g -big > bigoutput` creates only bigoutput. littleoutput is still missing. So make runs again for littleoutput. That's why twice.\n\nNow in our case: recipe `link /DLL /OUT:dll obj` creates BOTH dll and lib as side effects. So:\n- If make decides to run recipe for dll first: creates both. Then for lib: exists → skip. Once.\n- BUT the danger: make might decide to run the recipe for lib FIRST (if lib is the target needed, e.g. main.exe lists lib as prereq and not dll). Recipe runs, /OUT:dll — creates dll and lib. lib now exists. Then make checks: was the recipe run for dll too? Only if dll is also needed as a target.\n\nThe scenario where it runs twice: if BOTH dll and lib are needed, and make evaluates \"need to update\" for both BEFORE running either recipe. Does make do that? \n\nActually the answer: it depends. In parallel mode or when goals evaluated... Let me just recall real-world experience: The classic complaint is that with independent multi-target, the recipe CAN run multiple times. And this is precisely why `&:` was added.\n\nActually the real issue documented in NEWS 4.3 (grouped targets intro): \"Pattern rules have always had the ability to generate multiple targets with a single invocation of the recipe. It's now possible to declare that an explicit rule generates multiple targets with a single invocation.\"\n\nAnd the 4.4 NEWS: \"Previously each target in a explicit grouped target rule was considered individually: if the targets needed by the build were not out of date the recipe was not run even if other targets in the group were out of date. Now if any of the grouped targets are needed by the build, then if any of the grouped targets are out of date the recipe is run and all targets in the group are considered updated.\"\n\nSo for independent targets: each target considered individually; recipe runs for each target that needs updating.\n\nNow the crucial question for Irina: her rule lists dll as prereq of main.exe, and the recipe is shared. In HER makefile, main.exe depends on dll (not lib). The dll rule `dll lib &: obj` (grouped) — I'm proposing grouped. But currently she has the recipe attached to main.exe, not a dll/lib rule. Actually she has:\n```\n$(BINDIR)\\common\\common_cpp.dll: obj...\n\tlink /DLL /OUT:$@ $^\n```\nThat's a separate rule. And main.exe:\n```\nmain.exe: main.obj common_cpp.dll | ...\n\tset PATH\n\tlink ... main.obj common_cpp.lib\n```\nWait — main.exe depends on dll, not lib. And lib is a side-effect of dll. Her current approach is fine and does NOT have the multi-target problem at all! Because there's only ONE target (dll) in the rule, and lib is just a side effect.\n\nSo actually the double-execution issue only arises if she goes to multi-target `dll lib: obj` with classic `:` separator. My earlier claim \"if you use multi-target it runs twice\" needs precision.\n\nLet me be careful and accurate now. Let me re-derive precisely:\n\nScenario A (her current): dll is a normal target (rule `dll: objs`). main.exe prereq = dll. lib used only in recipe. Clean build: make needs main.exe → needs dll → dll missing → run dll recipe → creates dll + lib. main.exe links against lib. Works. No double-run. The only issue: if lib deleted but dll exists, make won't rebuild lib (since lib not a target). That's the \"incomplete graph\" caveat I mentioned.\n\nScenario B (multi-target `dll lib: objs`, classic `:`): This expands to two rules, same recipe. Now main.exe prereqs = dll AND lib (or just lib). If main.exe depends on lib: make needs lib → rule for lib → lib missing → run recipe (creates dll + lib as side effects, since /OUT:dll hardcoded... but $@=lib in recipe...). The recipe hardcodes /OUT:dll, so creates dll + its lib. Now lib exists. Does make also run for dll? Only if dll needed as goal/prereq. If main.exe depends only on lib, dll never built as a target, but its recipe created dll anyway (side effect). Runs once.\n\nIf main.exe depends on BOTH dll and lib: make processes prereqs in order. dll first → missing → run recipe → creates both. lib next → exists, fresh → skip. Runs once.\n\nWhen does it run TWICE? If make evaluates both as out-of-date at the same decision time before executing. In sequential make, the second check happens after the first recipe executed, so it sees the created file. So sequential: once.\n\nHmm, but there IS a documented double-run. The manual example: two targets bigoutput/littleoutput, recipe creates ONLY $@ (the specific target). Both missing → run once for bigoutput (creates bigoutput), again for littleoutput (creates littleoutput). Twice, because each run only creates one file. If a single run created both files, make wouldn't re-run.\n\nSo my earlier statement \"the recipe will run twice if both missing\" was WRONG for the case where the recipe creates both files as side effects! Let me correct myself honestly. This is exactly the anti-догmatism point — Irina's intuition is actually correct for sequential builds when a single recipe invocation creates both outputs.\n\nBUT there's still a real hazard with classic multi-target `dll lib: objs`: the recipe runs with $@ set to the specific target being built. If $@ is not used in the recipe, it's fine (recipe creates both). But if make needs to build `lib` and runs the recipe, it creates lib (side effect). Fine. The subtle risk: make might run the recipe for the WRONG target first, or run it multiple times in parallel builds (-j). In parallel mode with both dll and lib needed, both could be scheduled simultaneously → recipe runs twice concurrently → race condition / redundant work. That's the real reason for `&:`.\n\nSo the accurate picture:\n- Classic multi-target = sugar, expands to N rules with same recipe. Each target independently checked. Recipe runs for each target that needs updating at the time it's evaluated.\n- With sequential make and a recipe that creates all files: runs once (second check sees fresh file).\n- With parallel make or targets requested in different contexts: may run multiple times.\n- `&:` (grouped): recipe runs at most once, whenever any target in group is out of date; all treated as updated.\n\nLet me verify my claim \"second check sees the fresh file\" — is that how GNU make works? When make remakes a file, it checks timestamps. After dll recipe runs, lib file has a fresh timestamp. When make then considers lib target (if it's a separate rule in a multi-target), it checks: lib exists? yes. Is lib older than any prereq? obj older than lib → no. → up to date → skip. Yes, that's standard GNU make behavior.\n\nWait, but there's a subtlety about WHEN make evaluates \"need to update\". GNU make is a \"model of files\": for each goal it checks update status at the time it processes that goal. In a rule like `main.exe: dll lib`, make processes prereqs of main.exe in order: first dll (build it if needed), then lib (build it if needed). After dll built, lib exists. So skip. Yes.\n\nBUT actually — important! There's a known gotcha documented: if the recipe, when run for dll, creates lib, then for a multi-target rule make may still consider lib \"out of date\" if... no.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider whether GNU make caches the \"needs updating\" decision. There's a subtlety in GNU make: when make starts building, it records the status. Actually GNU make's algorithm: `update_file` checks whether the file needs updating by comparing timestamps. Each target checked independently when it becomes the current goal.\n\nOK here's the thing — I should be honest about the exact mechanism and not overclaim. The key correction to my earlier statement: the \"double execution\" is NOT guaranteed merely because both targets are missing; it happens specifically because each recipe run typically only updates the specific target ($@) it's building. When a single run updates all targets (as in the link case), sequential make runs it once. The real, robust reason to use `&:` is: (1) it tells make \"one invocation updates all\" so make never runs it twice even under parallel builds or when both targets are requested; (2) the semantics are what you actually mean.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider more carefully with the actual GNU make behavior, because I recall the manual's bigoutput example explicitly says both runs happen. Let me re-read the manual text I fetched:\n\n\"bigoutput littleoutput : text.g\n        generate text.g -$(subst output,,$@) > $@\n\nis equivalent to\n\nbigoutput : text.g\n        generate text.g -big > bigoutput\nlittleoutput : text.g\n        generate text.g -little > littleoutput\"\n\nSo with `make bigoutput littleoutput`, make runs recipe for bigoutput (creates bigoutput), then for littleoutput (creates littleoutput). Two invocations — but each creates only its own target. If instead the recipe were `generate all > bigoutput littleoutput` (creating both), make would run it once for bigoutput, then check littleoutput: exists & fresh → skip.\n\nSo Irina's intuition: \"recipe for dll runs first, lib appears; why run for lib again if lib already exists?\" — CORRECT for the link case! After dll's recipe creates lib, the lib target check finds it fresh → skip. Good — she's right, and I should confirm this and refine my earlier statement.\n\nNow, is there any case where the dll+lib multi-target would run twice in HER scenario? Let's see: Her rule would be:\n```\ndll lib: objs\n\tlink /DLL /OUT:dll $^\n```\nmain.exe depends on lib (in prereq list). Sequential: make needs lib → run recipe ($@=lib, but recipe hardcodes /OUT:dll) → creates dll + lib. lib fresh → done. Once.\n\nIf main.exe depended on both dll and lib: make processes dll first → run recipe → creates both → lib check: fresh → skip. Once.\n\nParallel -j: could both be scheduled? If both dll and lib are prereqs of main.exe, make schedules them in parallel potentially. Both might run the recipe concurrently → link invoked twice → race. That's the hazard.\n\nAlso there's another subtle thing: make doesn't know the recipe creates lib. If someone deletes ONLY lib (dll stays), make wants to rebuild lib → runs recipe → creates lib + dll (overwrites dll, fine). OK, that actually recovers. Hmm, so the \"incomplete graph\" concern I raised earlier is mitigated: with multi-target `dll lib: objs`, lib IS a target with a rule, so deleting lib triggers rebuild. That's actually BETTER than her current setup where lib is not a target at all (deleting lib breaks the build with \"cannot open .lib\").\n\nWait, with her current setup (dll is the only target, lib only referenced in main.exe recipe): if lib deleted but dll exists, main.exe rebuild (if triggered) fails with \"cannot open common_cpp.lib\". Because nothing rebuilds lib. Yes.\n\nWith classic multi-target `dll lib: objs`: lib is a target. Deleting lib → make sees lib missing → runs the rule → link creates both. Recovers. But risk of double-run in parallel.\n\nWith grouped `dll lib &: objs`: single rule, recipe runs once when any of dll/lib out of date; both marked updated. Recovers from deleted lib, no double-run, parallel-safe. This is the correct solution.\n\nSo the honest, precise answer to Irina:\n\n1. Your intuition is right for sequential builds: if the recipe for dll creates lib too, make checks lib afterwards and finds it fresh → does NOT run a second time.\n\n2. My earlier \"runs twice\" statement was imprecise. The double-run in the manual's example happens because that recipe only creates the specific $@ target each time, so the second target is still missing. If one invocation creates all outputs, sequential make runs it once.\n\n3. The REAL reasons to use `&:` grouped targets instead of classic multi-target or your current setup:\n   - Correct graph: lib becomes a real node; deleting lib triggers rebuild (your current setup fails in that case).\n   - One recipe invocation guaranteed even under parallel builds (-j) — with classic multi-target and both targets needed, make could invoke the recipe once per target, possibly concurrently → redundant/racy link.\n   - Semantics match intent: \"one command produces several files\".\n\n4. So: your current rule is fine for the happy path, and its only weakness is lib not being a graph node. If you switch to multi-target, use `&:` (make 4.4.1 supports it), NOT classic `:`.\n\nLet me also double check: does classic multi-target run the recipe once per target \"that needs updating\", evaluated when? I want to be accurate. Actually GNU make manual 4.10 doesn't explicitly say \"once per target that needs updating\". Let me check... The manual text I got doesn't contain that phrase in the excerpt. The 4.4 NEWS says for grouped targets \"Previously each target in a explicit grouped target rule was considered individually: if the targets needed by the build were not out of date the recipe was not run even if other targets in the group were out of date.\" — that's the 4.4 change describing old grouped-target behavior. For independent targets (classic `:`), the recipe is duplicated; each duplicated rule is independent.\n\nI think the accurate framing: with classic `:`, the rule is literally duplicated into N independent rules. Whether the recipe runs N times depends on how many of those N targets need updating. If the recipe's single invocation creates all N, then after the first, the rest are fresh → skipped. If the recipe only creates $@, all N missing → N invocations.\n\nI'm fairly confident. Let me also verify my claim about parallel builds and classic multi-target running twice. With `make -j2 main.exe` where main.exe depends on dll and lib, and rule `dll lib: objs`: make will schedule dll and lib as parallel prerequisites. Both missing → both \"needs updating\" → both run the recipe → two concurrent links writing to same files. This is a real race. Yes, I'm confident that's the documented behavior rationale.\n\nActually, hmm — does make, when it sees two targets of the SAME rule, treat them specially? No — classic multi-target just duplicates the rule; make has no idea they're related. So parallel: both run. Yes.\n\nNow let me answer concisely in Russian, correcting my earlier overstatement honestly (anti-sycophancy: admit I was imprecise). Structure:\n\n1. Твоя интуиция верна — для твоего случая рецепт НЕ выполнится дважды. Разбор почему.\n2. Почему в manual-примере выполняется дважды (там рецепт создаёт только $@).\n3. Уточнение моего прошлого утверждения — оно было неточным.\n4. Тогда зачем `&:`? — удалённый lib, параллельная сборка.\n5. Вывод: твой текущий вариант рабочий; если multi-target — только `&:`.\n\nI should keep it focused. This is still plan mode, discussing. No file edits.\n\nLet me write the answer.",
