ke runs a recipe, it invokes something like `cmd.exe /c \"set \"PATH=%~dp0\\build\\debug\\common;%PATH%\" && ...\"`. In that context, `%~dp0` refers to... hmm. Actually, when cmd /c is run from make, the command line passed is the whole recipe. `%0` would be... Let me think.\n\nActually in `cmd /c \"command\"`, the command string is treated as if it were `%0`? No. When you do `cmd /c \"something\"`, %0 is not set in a useful way. `%~dp0` expands to the drive and path of %0. If %0 is empty, then %~dp0 might expand to something weird or empty.\n\nActually, let me recall: `cmd /c \"set \"PATH=%~dp0\\build;%PATH%\" && echo %PATH%\"`. What is %0 here? Hmm, this is tricky. In cmd /c, %0 is typically... I recall that when the command is given as a string argument, `%0` is set to the string? Actually no.\n\nLet me think about how make invokes recipes on Windows. GNU make on Windows uses cmd.exe with `/c` and the whole recipe. When make joins multiple lines with `&&`, it creates a single command line. Actually here the recipe has continuation lines with `\\`.\n\nSo make runs: `cmd.exe /c \"set \"PATH=%~dp0\\build\\debug\\common;%PATH%\" && build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe \"...\" 66 && ... && type nul > build\\debug\\test\\t3_cpp_empty_case.ok\"`\n\nInside this cmd instance, `%~dp0` — %0 would be... In `cmd /c \"string\"`, %0 is empty or the cmd.exe name? Actually when cmd is invoked with /c and a command string, %0 is not defined as a path. Let me recall: `cmd /c \"%~dp0\"` — I believe it prints nothing or errors. \n\nHmm, actually the deeper issue: the error is 0xC0000135 (STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND). This is from main.exe failing to load common_cpp.dll. So the PATH didn't help. Why? Because either:\n1. `%~dp0` expanded to empty/wrong, so PATH didn't get the common dir prepended. OR\n2. The PATH was set in the outer cmd, but run_case.exe spawns a new cmd.exe via std::system — which inherits the environment, so PATH would be inherited correctly. So that shouldn't be the issue IF PATH was set correctly.\n3. Encoding issue: `set \"PATH=...\"` — hmm.\n\nActually wait, let me reconsider. `%PATH%` inside the recipe — when make expands the recipe line, does `%PATH%` survive? Make doesn't expand `%`, it's not a make variable. So `%PATH%` stays literally and is expanded by cmd. That's fine.\n\nBut `%~dp0` — make passes it to cmd verbatim. Now, cmd.exe with `/c`: when you have `cmd /c \"set \"PATH=%~dp0\\...\"`... \n\nLet me think about what %0 is in `cmd /c \"command string\"`. I recall that for `cmd /c`, if you give a command string, %0 is empty string, and %~dp0 expands to empty. Actually, let me recall more carefully. There's a known gotcha: `%~dp0` only works in batch files. In a cmd /c one-liner, %0 = empty, so %~dp0 = empty. Then PATH = `\\build\\debug\\common;%PATH%`? Wait, if %~dp0 is empty, then PATH becomes `\\build\\debug\\common;<old PATH>`. Hmm, a path starting with backslash `\\build\\...` relative to current drive root. That wouldn't point to the right place. Actually it would be `\\build\\debug\\common` on current drive root, which isn't where common_cpp.dll is.\n\nHmm wait, but is %~dp0 empty in cmd /c? Let me think. Actually there's subtlety: in `cmd /c`, `%0` is set to... I've seen that in a pipe or with /c, %0 can be empty. Actually, I recall that `%~dp0` in cmd /c \"...\" gives empty because %0 is not a valid path. So PATH prepends `\\build\\debug\\common` (relative to root of current drive), not `C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\inobitec_stud\\build\\debug\\common`. So the DLL path is wrong → main.exe fails to load common_cpp.dll → STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND (0xC0000135 = -1073741515).\n\nBut wait, in the .bat files, `set \"PATH=%~dp0/build/common;%PATH%\"` worked because it WAS a batch file, where %~dp0 is the directory of the batch file.\n\nSo the hint: `%~dp0` is batch-file syntax and doesn't work in make recipes (cmd /c context). This is the likely culprit. The user says \"дело не в слешах - пробовала по-разному. дело в чём-то другом\". She thinks it's not about slashes. Let me verify the hypothesis more carefully though.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether the DLL-not-found hypothesis is right. Exit code -1073741515 = 0xC0000135. Yes, 0xC0000135 is STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND. The error message in cmd would be \"The application failed to initialize properly (0xc0000135)\". And \"Ожидался код: 66. Был получен код: -1073741515\" — run_case compares real_code (from std::system return) with 66. std::system on Windows returns the exit code as given by the process; for a failed DLL load, the process terminates with 0xC0000135 as the exit code, which is negative in signed 32-bit.\n\nSo main.exe failed to start because common_cpp.dll was not found. Why not found? Because PATH was set incorrectly due to %~dp0 not expanding in cmd /c context.\n\nActually, let me double check %~dp0 in cmd /c. Hmm. I want to be careful. Let me recall: `cmd /c \"echo %~dp0\"`. I believe this outputs nothing (empty). Actually, I've tested this before... In `cmd /c \"echo %0\"` → outputs nothing? Or maybe it outputs the first token?\n\nActually, there's another possibility. When make invokes a recipe with multiple lines joined, it may use a temporary batch file? No, GNU make on Windows runs cmd /c with the recipe. Actually, for multi-line recipes, make joins lines and runs as one command with the shell. On Windows with cmd, make uses `cmd.exe /c \"...\"` or writes a temp .bat file? Let me recall. GNU make on Windows uses cmd.exe with /c and the whole recipe joined. When the recipe is long, it may hit command-line length limits.\n\nHmm, but actually there's an important subtlety about make and cmd: GNU make on Windows does NOT pass the entire multi-line recipe as one command; each logical line of the recipe (even joined with `\\` continuation) — make joins continuation lines into one line, then runs that one line via `cmd.exe /c <line>`? Actually make passes the whole recipe... Let me think again.\n\nGNU make executes each recipe line separately via the shell (cmd.exe /c \"line\"). Continuation with `\\` at end joins lines before execution. So the joined line is: `set \"PATH=%~dp0\\build\\debug\\common;%PATH%\" && build\\...\\run_case.exe \"...\" 66 && ...`.\n\ncmd /c \"set \"PATH=...&&...\"` — here's another problem: nested quotes. `cmd /c \"set \"PATH=...` — the outer quotes... Actually make on Windows wraps the whole command in quotes: `cmd.exe /c \"set \"PATH=%~dp0\\build\\debug\\common;%PATH%\" && build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe \"build\\debug\\t3_bbox_cpp\\main.exe < ... > ...\" 66 && ...\"`. The quoting could break, but user says she tried different slashes; error is DLL not found.\n\nThe cleanest answer: %~dp0 doesn't work in make recipe context (it's a batch-file variable), so PATH was not set to the right directory, so main.exe couldn't find common_cpp.dll → STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND (0xC0000135 = -1073741515). \n\nAlso, even if %~dp0 worked, there's the deeper architectural point: she shouldn't rely on PATH at all in the recipe. Better approaches:\n1. Use `$(CURDIR)` or the makefile's directory: `set \"PATH=$(CURDIR)\\build\\debug\\common;%PATH%\"`. But $(CURDIR) is make-relative. Actually $(CURDIR) gives current working dir of make. That works but is brittle.\n2. Better: run main.exe with the dll found via a wrapper that sets PATH... or\n3. Better architectural solution: don't use PATH; instead copy common_cpp.dll into the exe's directory (Windows looks for DLLs in the exe's dir first), or use a load-point. Hmm, but that's a change.\n\nActually, the cleanest for the course: since each recipe is its own cmd, and she needs DLL resolution at runtime, options:\n- Set PATH using make variable: `set \"PATH=$(CURDIR)\\build\\$(CONFIG)\\common;%PATH%\"` — hmm, but $(CURDIR) is the make invocation dir.\n- Or better: define a make variable `COMMON_BIN := $(CURDIR)\\$(BINDIR)\\common` and use it.\n- Or: instead of PATH, copy dll to the test dir / exe dir.\n\nBut wait — the user is in plan mode and asking for a hint (\"дай мне наводку, почему так происходит\"). She wants the reason, not a full solution. Per AGENTS.md §4.10, she should formulate hypotheses and diagnose. I should give her a strong hint: the exit code -1073741515 = 0xC0000135 = STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND → main.exe didn't start because it couldn't load common_cpp.dll → PATH set in the recipe didn't take effect / pointed wrong → and `%~dp0` is batch-file-only syntax that doesn't expand in a make recipe (cmd /c context).\n\nLet me verify 0xC0000135 = -1073741515 numerically.\n0xC0000135 = 12*16^7 = 12*268435456 = 3221225472; plus 0x135 = 309. So 3221225472+309 = 3221225781. As signed 32-bit: 3221225781 - 4294967296 = -1073741515. Yes! Confirmed.\n\nSTATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND = 0xC0000135. Yes.\n\nAlso the STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND is commonly seen when running an exe that depends on a DLL that isn't findable — e.g., debug CRT dlls (msvcp140d.dll etc.) or her common_cpp.dll. Given /MDd (debug CRT) — actually the debug CRT (msvcp140d.dll, ucrtbased.dll) is not normally on PATH! Wait, that's important. /MDd links against debug versions of the CRT. When running via the \"x64 Native Tools Command Prompt\", PATH includes the debug CRT directory? Hmm. Actually, the debug CRT is found via... no. When you compile with /MDd and run outside VS, you get errors about missing msvcp140d.dll / vcruntime140d.dll / ucrtbased.dll. But she's running in the Native Tools prompt — does that add debug CRT to PATH? The vsdevcmd adds a bunch to PATH including VC runtime directories. I believe the Native Tools prompt PATH includes the vc redist debug dir? Hmm, actually the debug CRT is installed with VS and the prompt might not add it. But her .bat tests worked (she ran t3_build_and_test.bat successfully). So in the batch, main.exe ran fine with the PATH set to build\\common. That means common_cpp.dll was the missing piece in the batch, and with PATH set, it worked. And debug CRT must be findable in her environment (maybe ucrtbased is in System32 or the prompt sets it).\n\nSo the difference between .bat (works) and make recipe (fails with 0xC0000135): the PATH set. In .bat, %~dp0 = batch file dir = repo root, so PATH gets `C:\\...\\inobitec_stud\\build\\common;...` → common_cpp.dll found → works. In make recipe, %~dp0 expands to empty (or garbage) because %0 isn't a batch file → PATH gets `\\build\\debug\\common;...` (relative to current drive root) → common_cpp.dll not found → 0xC0000135.\n\nWait, but actually I should double-check: does `%~dp0` in a `cmd /c \"...\"` context expand to empty? Let me recall a known behavior: `cmd /c \"echo %~dp0\"` — I believe %0 in this context is... Hmm. When cmd starts with /c, %0 is set to the command name? Let me think. If you run `cmd /c \"echo %0\"`, I'm fairly sure %0 is empty (or the literal?). There's a well-known gotcha that `%~dp0` doesn't work in `cmd /c`, only in batch files. Yes, I'm fairly confident: %~dp0 is undefined (empty) in cmd /c one-liners, because %0 refers to the batch file name which doesn't exist in this invocation.\n\nActually, I want to double-check whether GNU make on Windows uses cmd /c or a temp batch file for multi-line recipes. For a multi-line recipe, GNU make Windows: it concatenates lines and passes to cmd /c. I don't think it uses temp .bat for regular recipes. There was a thing where make uses temp batch files when the command contains `\"` in certain ways? Hmm. In GNU make 4.x, on Windows, recipes are run via cmd.exe with the entire recipe passed as a single command line. Actually the manual says: \"When a recipe is a long line... On MS-Windows...\" I recall that GNU make writes a temporary .bat file for recipes that are too long for the command line limit. But normally not.\n\nRegardless, the key diagnostic fact is the exit code 0xC0000135 = DLL not found. And the likely cause is PATH not pointing at build\\common. Since she \"tried different slashes\" but it's about %~dp0 semantics, not slashes.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: could it be that %~dp0 DID expand but to a wrong path? If make runs cmd /c from the working directory (repo root), and %~dp0 expanded to something... If %0 is empty, %~dp0 is empty → PATH = `\\build\\debug\\common;...` → this resolves to `C:\\build\\debug\\common` (current drive root) → not found. Either way: wrong path.\n\nAlternatively maybe the issue is entirely different: nested quoting of `run_case.exe \"...\"` combined with `&&` inside cmd /c might break the PATH set? No — the error shows run_case did run and got code -1073741515 (it printed \"Ожидался код: 66. Был получен код: -1073741515\"), which is run_case's own stderr message. So run_case.exe ran fine, PATH set line ran (no error). The inner main.exe failed to start with 0xC0000135. So the PATH set to the wrong dir.\n\nSo the hint: -1073741515 = 0xC0000135 = STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND. main.exe couldn't load common_cpp.dll at startup. The `set PATH` line didn't point to the right directory, because `%~dp0` is a **batch-file-only** variable: it expands %0's drive+path, and %0 is only defined inside a .bat file. In a make recipe (cmd /c context) %0 isn't the batch file, so %~dp0 expands to empty → PATH got `\\build\\debug\\common` (drive-root-relative) → dll not found.\n\nBut wait — I should double check: in the recipe, is it `%~dp0\\$(BINDIR)\\common` where $(BINDIR)=build\\debug? So PATH = `<%~dp0>build\\debug\\common`? There's no backslash between %~dp0 and build. In .bat, %~dp0 ends with a backslash (C:\\...\\inobitec_stud\\), so `%~dp0build\\...` = `C:\\...\\inobitec_stud\\build\\...`. OK. In make recipe, if %~dp0 = empty → PATH = `build\\debug\\common;...`? Wait if %~dp0 is empty then it's `build\\debug\\common;...` relative to current dir = repo root → that would actually resolve correctly IF make's cwd is repo root! Hmm. But wait, does cmd resolve relative PATH entries relative to current dir? Yes, PATH entries without drive get resolved relative to the current directory at the time of search. If cwd is repo root, `build\\debug\\common` would be found... \n\nHmm, so if %~dp0 is empty, PATH = `build\\debug\\common;<old>` and cwd = repo root → common_cpp.dll at `build\\debug\\common\\common_cpp.dll` → should be found! Unless the dll isn't built there. Wait — is the dll built? Let me check the build dir. Actually the makefile builds `$(BINDIR)\\common\\common_cpp.dll` = `build\\debug\\common\\common_cpp.dll`. And the PATH entry would be `build\\debug\\common`. That should work IF cwd is repo root.\n\nBut hold on — the exit code is DLL_NOT_FOUND. So either the dll wasn't there, or PATH entry was wrong. Hmm. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually wait: is common_cpp.dll actually built? In the makefile, the dll rule exists (line 193). And t3_cpp exe rule depends on it. But has she built the dll? If she's building the test for the first time, the dll rule should have run as prerequisite. Unless... the dll rule uses grouped targets now? She said \"ok. grouped target + жесткий путь до длл - буду делать так.\" — she said she WILL do grouped target + hardcoded path. So she hasn't necessarily changed the makefile yet; she might be describing her plan.\n\nActually the failing command shows `set \"PATH=%~dp0\\build\\debug\\common;%PATH%\" && ...`. So this is in the test recipe. The test depends on the exe and dll being built.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider whether %~dp0 expands to empty in cmd /c. Actually, I recall now: for `cmd /c \"command\"`, %0 IS set to... hmm. Let me think about `cmd /c echo %0`. I genuinely believe %0 is empty here. There's a well-known Stack Overflow answer: \"%~dp0 only works in batch files, not in cmd /c\" — because %0 is the batch file name.\n\nBUT, there's a subtlety with make on Windows: make might pass the recipe via a temporary batch file if the command contains certain characters. If make uses a temp .bat, then %0 = temp bat path → %~dp0 = temp dir → wrong anyway.\n\nLet me reconsider the whole thing differently. The user said \"дело не в слешах - пробовала по-разному. дело в чём-то другом. дай мне наводку\". So she wants a hint about the real cause.\n\nThe real cause, based on exit code: 0xC0000135 STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND. main.exe couldn't load common_cpp.dll. So the PATH manipulation didn't result in build\\common being searchable. Why? Two candidate reasons:\n1. `%~dp0` is batch-only → expands to empty (or garbage) → PATH points somewhere wrong.\n2. Nested quoting issue with `set \"PATH=...\"` inside `cmd /c \"set \"PATH=...` — the outer quotes of cmd /c might break the inner set. Actually when make runs `cmd /c`, and the command itself contains quotes, make on Windows quotes it. The whole thing: `cmd /c \"set \"PATH=%~dp0\\build\\debug\\common;%PATH%\" && ...\"`. cmd parses this: /c then the rest. The first quote after /c is stripped. Then it sees `set \"PATH=%~dp0\\build\\debug\\common;%PATH%\"` — the set value is quoted... then `&& ...`. This usually works.\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider: maybe the issue is that run_case.exe uses std::system which launches `cmd /c <command>` — a NEW cmd. That new cmd inherits the parent's environment (PATH with build\\common prepended) IF the set command took effect in the parent. But if the set command was part of the same && chain and the quoting broke, PATH might not have been set.\n\nBut she gets run_case's message, meaning run_case ran and its child (main.exe) failed with 0xC0000135. So the child process inherited whatever PATH the recipe cmd had. If set worked and PATH had correct dir, main.exe would find the dll. It didn't → PATH didn't have correct dir.\n\nThe most likely and clean explanation: `%~dp0` doesn't expand in make recipes (cmd /c), it's batch-file syntax. So PATH was set to something wrong (empty prefix → `build\\debug\\common` relative... which would actually work from repo root; OR the temp-batch scenario gives a temp dir).\n\nHold on, let me reconsider the empty case more carefully. If %~dp0 → empty, PATH = `build\\debug\\common;<old>`. When Windows searches PATH for common_cpp.dll, for a relative PATH entry it uses the process's current directory. The recipe's cmd runs with cwd = make's cwd = repo root (assuming make invoked from repo root). So `build\\debug\\common` → repo_root\\build\\debug\\common → dll found. So that would work, contradicting DLL_NOT_FOUND.\n\nUnless make's cwd is NOT repo root? The user runs make in repo root presumably. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe %~dp0 does NOT expand to empty but to something like `C:\\Program Files\\...` or the make binary path? Actually in cmd /c context, %0... Let me just recall concretely. I've seen: running `cmd /c \"echo [%0]\"` gives `[]` (empty). Running `cmd /c \"%0\"` errors. I'm fairly confident %0 is empty in cmd /c with a command string. So %~dp0 = empty.\n\nSo then PATH = `build\\debug\\common;...` and cwd = repo root → should find dll. So why DLL_NOT_FOUND?\n\nAlternative: maybe the dll is NOT actually built as `build\\debug\\common\\common_cpp.dll`. Perhaps she's testing a scenario where the dll rule didn't run. Let me check: does the t3 cpp test recipe depend on the exe? She wrote it depends on `$(T3_CPP_EXE)` presumably, which depends on the dll. Hmm.\n\nActually — WAIT. Let me reconsider. The message: \"Ожидался код: 66. Был получен код: -1073741515\". The test is `t3_cpp_empty_case.ok`. The empty case expects code 66 (no input). But instead of main.exe returning 66, it crashed at startup with 0xC0000135. So main.exe didn't even start properly. That's DLL not found — either common_cpp.dll or a debug CRT dll.\n\nDebug CRT: /MDd links against msvcp140d.dll, vcruntime140d.dll, ucrtbased.dll. These are NOT typically on PATH. When you run from the Native Tools prompt, does PATH include them? The vcvars sets PATH to include VC redist? Actually, the debug CRT DLLs are in the VS installation directory (e.g., C:\\Program Files\\Microsoft Visual Studio\\...\\VC\\Redist\\MSVC\\<ver>\\debug_nonredist\\x64\\Microsoft.VC143.DebugCRT\\). Does vcvars64.bat add that to PATH? I believe the x64 Native Tools prompt does add debug_nonredist to PATH for running debug apps! Yes, I think vcvars adds the debug CRT path so debug apps run from the prompt. That's why her .bat tests worked: she ran them from the Native Tools prompt, debug CRT was on PATH, and she added build\\common to PATH for the dll.\n\nSo in the make recipe, both the debug CRT path (from the prompt env) and build\\common should be inherited... unless make is launched from a different environment, or the `set PATH=` line is fine.\n\nOK here's another thought: maybe the actual problem is the QUOTING, not the PATH content. When the recipe cmd is `cmd /c \"set \"PATH=%~dp0\\build\\debug\\common;%PATH%\" && ...\"`, make on Windows... Actually, GNU make on Windows, when invoking cmd.exe, quotes the command. The nested quotes in `run_case.exe \"build\\debug\\t3_bbox_cpp\\main.exe < ... > ... 2> nul\"` — the inner command has quotes for the stdin redirection path? No, the quoted string is the whole inner command. Inside cmd /c, `build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe \"build\\debug\\t3_bbox_cpp\\main.exe < tests\\... > ...\"` — run_case's argv[1] is the quoted command. That should be fine; run_case did run (it printed the error message), so argv parsing worked.\n\nSo run_case ran, and std::system launched `cmd /c \"build\\debug\\t3_bbox_cpp\\main.exe < tests\\input_data\\t3_empty_case > build\\debug\\t3_bbox_cpp\\t3_empty_case 2> nul\"`. This child cmd inherits env PATH from the recipe cmd. If the recipe's `set PATH` succeeded and prepended the right dir, main.exe would find the dll.\n\nGiven DLL_NOT_FOUND, the PATH set didn't produce a working search path. The most teachable explanation is the %~dp0 issue. But I showed the empty case would still work from repo root... unless the dll is not there.\n\nHmm, let me actually check whether the dll exists and where. But I'm in plan mode (read-only) — I can inspect! Let me check the build directory. Also check if maybe the PATH entry with empty %~dp0 becomes `\\build\\debug\\common` (leading backslash → root of current drive) — wait! If %~dp0 is empty and the line is `set \"PATH=%~dp0\\build\\debug\\common;%PATH%\"`, then it's `\\build\\debug\\common;...` — LEADING BACKSLASH! Because there's `%~dp0\\build` — the `\\build` after empty %~dp0 → PATH = `\\build\\debug\\common;<old>`. A PATH entry starting with `\\` is drive-root relative → `C:\\build\\debug\\common` → NOT repo root! That's the bug!\n\nIn the .bat, %~dp0 = `C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\inobitec_stud\\` (ends with backslash), so `%~dp0\\build\\...`? Wait, in the bat it was `set \"PATH=%~dp0/build/common;%PATH%\"` — forward slashes. In the make recipe she wrote `%~dp0\\build\\debug\\common`. If %~dp0 = `C:\\...\\inobitec_stud\\` then `%~dp0\\build` = `C:\\...\\inobitec_stud\\\\build` (double backslash, fine). But in cmd /c, %~dp0 = empty → `\\build\\debug\\common` → drive root. That's the classic gotcha!\n\nSo my hint: `%~dp0` expands to the batch file's directory and is defined ONLY inside .bat files. In a make recipe (cmd /c context), %0 is empty → %~dp0 = empty → your PATH becomes `\\build\\debug\\common;...` which cmd interprets as `C:\\build\\debug\\common` (root of current drive), NOT your project dir. So common_cpp.dll isn't found → main.exe fails to start → exit code 0xC0000135 (STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND) = -1073741515.\n\nThat's a clean, correct, teachable explanation. And it matches \"дело не в слешах\" — she tried forward/back slashes, but the real issue is %~dp0 semantics.\n\nThe fix (for later, in execution phase): don't use %~dp0 in make. Use make's own variables. GNU make has `$(CURDIR)` = current directory (absolute). And the dll dir = `$(CURDIR)\\$(BINDIR)\\common`. So `set \"PATH=$(CURDIR)\\$(BINDIR)\\common;%PATH%\"`. That's portable enough and uses make's path knowledge. Also note she shouldn't hardcode build\\debug in recipes since CONFIG is variable — use $(BINDIR).\n\nBut actually there's a subtlety with $(CURDIR) and make on Windows