nt.\n\nLet me write the response in Russian, concise but with the teaching value. Mark categories and confidence per her protocol: [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ] etc.\n\nOne more consideration: she wrote \"set \"PATH=%~dp0\\$(BINDIR)\\common;%PATH%\" && \\ build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe ...\". The `&& \\` then newline then the command. In make, backslash-newline is line continuation. Fine.\n\nAlso note: even with $(BINDIR) inside a set for cmd, make expands $(BINDIR) to build\\debug — good, that part works (make variables are expanded before cmd sees it). Only %~dp0 (cmd variable) is the problem.\n\nAlso, another subtle point: since CONFIG is a variable, and the test .ok files are in $(TESTDIR) = build\\debug\\test, all consistent.\n\nLet me verify the exit code theory and %~dp0 empirically (read-only), then respond.\n\nActually, I realize I should double-check: does GNU make on Windows run recipes via `cmd /c` or `cmd /s /c`? The quoting differs but %~dp0 behavior is the same: no batch file → %0 empty. Actually with /s /c \"...\" %0 might be set to the command string's first word? Let me recall. With `cmd /s /c \"echo %0\"`... Hmm. I recall that `cmd /c` sets %0 to... Actually there IS a documented behavior: when you do `cmd /c \"command\"`, the command string is passed, and %0 is not a filename. Some sources say %0 = \"cmd.exe\" or empty. Let me just empirically test both forms.\n\nI'll run:\n- `cmd /c \"echo [%0] [%~dp0]\"`\n- `cmd /s /c \"echo [%0] [%~dp0]\"`\n\nThese are read-only. Good.\n\nLet me also consider: could the issue be that the recipe cmd is run from a temp directory? No, make runs from cwd.\n\nLet me run the checks.",
