und → 0xC0000135. So the test fails.\n\nSo the hint: `%~dp0` is a batch-file-only variable. In a make recipe, make invokes cmd.exe directly (not through a .bat), so %0/%1/%~dp0 etc. don't expand. The correct way in make: make has its own variables. `$(CURDIR)` = current directory of make. Or use `$(BINDIR)\\common` relative. Actually the recipe already references `build\\debug\\...` explicitly, so it's run from the project root. So PATH could just be `set \"PATH=build\\debug\\common;%PATH%\"` (relative), or better use make variables: `$(BINDIR)\\common`.\n\nWait, but the user said \"и дело не в слешах - пробовала по-разному\". Yes, it's not slashes — it's the `%~dp0` batch variable.\n\nLet me confirm the exit code mapping: 0xC0000135. Actually let me double check: 0xC0000000 = 3221225472. 0x135 = 309. So 0xC0000135 = 3221225472 + 309 = 3221225781. 3221225781 - 2^32 = 3221225781 - 4294967296 = -1073741515. Yes! Confirmed. 0xC0000135 = STATUS_DLL_NOT_FOUND.\n\nGreat, so my diagnosis is solid. Also I should give the hint rather than the full solution per teaching style, but I can verify. The user asked \"дай мне наводку, почему так происходит\" — give a hint.\n\nLet me verify that the dll actually exists in build\\debug\\common. Let me check the build\\debug\\common directory.",
