nded correctly? `%PATH%` is expanded by cmd when the line is parsed. Since it's all one command line, `%PATH%` expands to the old PATH at parse time. Good. And `$(CURDIR)\\$(BINDIR)\\common` is expanded by make at make-parse time. Good.\n\nOne potential problem: `PATH` value could contain `&` characters? Unlikely here.\n\n6. **Check `run_case.exe` behavior with the empty case:** The test `t3_cpp_empty_case` expects exit code 66 (NO_INPUT). Program returns `exit_code::no_in` = 66. But wait, `run_case` runs `$(T3_CPP_EXE) < input > nul 2> file`. Fine.\n\n7. **Potential issue: the empty case test for both C and C++ versions use the same input and expect files.** For empty input, C version returns `no_input` (66), C++ returns `exit_code::no_in` (66). Same message? C: \"Пустые данные отсутствуют\" (from main.c line 104). C++: \"Пустые данные отсутствуют\" (main.cpp line 52). Wait, C main.c says `fprintf(stderr, \"������� ������ �����������\\n\")` — that's \"Пустые данные отсутствуют\". C++ main.cpp line 52 `std::cerr << \"������� ������ �����������\\n\"` — same. OK.\n\n8. **Trailing spaces / exact message match for `too_few_args`:** C main.c has `\"������ %d. ��������� ���������� X Y Z. ��������: %s\\n\"` vs expect `t3_too_few_args_case` (51 bytes): \"Строка 1. Не хватает координат X Y Z. Найдено: 2 3\". Hmm, but earlier in the bat for C version there was a trailing space issue: `findstr /C:\"... 2 3 \"` — the C version's parse_point may output a trailing space because of the way it splits. Let me check.\n\nActually earlier journal noted: C version too_few_args expected \"2 3 \" with trailing space in the bat (line 177 in the bat: `\"������ 1. ��������� ���������� X Y Z. ��������: 2 3 \"`). But the expect file is 51 bytes. Let me check whether expect has trailing space.\n\nThe expect t3_too_few_args_case = 51 bytes. Message \"Строка 1. Не хватает координат X Y Z. Найдено: 2 3\" — let me count in CP1251. \"Строка 1. Не хватает координат X Y Z. Найдено: 2 3\" — all Cyrilli