000\nСреднее расстояние от точек до центроида: 16.000\n```\nThe expect t3_test3 = 223 bytes. Program stdout → file in text mode → CRLF. Expect has CRLF. Good.\n\n10. **Wait — a real potential bug: `$(SET_DLL_PATH)` combined with `&&` and make's command-line limits.** The recipe lines are long. cmd handles it. Fine.\n\n11. **Grouped targets `&:` and `$^`:** In the grouped rule:\n```\n$(BINDIR)\\common\\common_cpp.dll $(BINDIR)\\common\\common_cpp.lib &: $(CPP_DLL_OBJ) | $(BINDIR)\\common\n\t$(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) /DLL /OUT:$(BINDIR)\\common\\common_cpp.dll $^\n```\n`$^` = the two objs. Good. `/OUT:` hardcoded to dll. Good — this is the correct approach they discussed.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtle issue with the grouped target and the `main.exe` rule. The main.exe rule depends on `$(BINDIR)\\common\\common_cpp.dll` (line 226). But the recipe links with `common_cpp.lib`. The lib is produced by the grouped rule when dll is rebuilt. But what if the `.lib` is deleted while `.dll` is fresh? The main.exe target depends only on the `.dll`, not on `.lib`. If `.lib` is missing, make sees main.exe older than dll → relinks → link fails because `.lib` missing, and the grouped rule won't re-run because `.dll` is up-to-date and nothing depends on `.lib`.\n\nThis is exactly the \"неполный граф\" issue we discussed! The user kept the dependency on `.dll` in the main.exe rule. With grouped targets, they should depend on `.lib` (or both). Because the thing main.exe actually consumes at link time is the `.lib`. The `.dll` is only needed at runtime (PATH). So the prerequisite for main.exe should be `common_cpp.lib`, not `common_cpp.dll`. Then if `.lib` is deleted, the grouped rule re-runs and recreates both. That's the honest graph.\n\nBut note: the PATH setting at runtime still needs the dll. The dll is a grouped sibling of lib, so rebuilding lib also refreshes dll. And at runtime PATH points to the common dir. So depending on lib is correct and sufficient. This is a genuine improvement to flag.\n\nActually wait — but there's the runtime dependency too. main.exe needs the dll at runtime. The build graph models compile-time/link-time dependencies. Runtime needs are handled by PATH. Since grouped targets treat them as one unit, depending on lib is enough for the build graph: rebuilding lib rebuilds dll too. So recommend changing prerequisite from `common_cpp.dll` to `common_cpp.lib` (or adding lib). Good catch.\n\n12. **`T3_CPP_OBJ` and `T3_C_OBJ` are commented out / not defined.** They're not used (the exe rules use explicit obj paths). Dead comment. Minor.\n\n13. **Duplicate variable definitions (58-59) unused.** Should be removed. Dead code.\n\n14. **Missing mass tests with gen_cloud** — per §6.1 task requirement. The generator is built but not wired into any test. This is the biggest gap vs. the task statement. But the user may be planning it next. I'll flag.\n\n15. **`test` target depends on `format-check`** which uses `$(wildcard **/*.cpp ...)`. The `**` in GNU make wildcard — does it work recursively? In GNU make, `$(wildcard **/*.cpp)` — `**` is not special; `*` doesn't cross directory boundaries, so `**/*.cpp` is effectively `*/*.cpp`? Actually `**` as glob: `*` matches any chars except `/`. So `**/*.cpp` = one level deep. That covers t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.cpp etc. But gen_cloud\\main.cpp is one level deep too. tests\\*.cpp one level. common\\*.cpp one level. So `**/*.cpp` catches all current sources one level deep. But not two levels. Fine for now. Not t3-specific.\n\nActually wait — does `$(wildcard **/*.cpp)` on Windows GNU make work with backslashes? wildcard uses forward-slash globs; on Windows, GNU make's wildcard handles both. It works since format works (user said everything green). OK.\n\n16. **Encoding consistency:** The Makefile itself is in CP1251 (comments garbled when read as UTF-8). The .editorconfig specifies charset? Let me check. Earlier journal said editorconfig charset couldn't be CP1251 and left unset, notes converte