he full command string. When run_case spawns `cmd /c \"...\"`, does the child inherit the PATH that was set in the recipe's cmd session? Yes — the recipe's `set PATH=...` modifies the environment of the current cmd.exe process, and children spawned by it (run_case.exe, and run_case's child cmd) inherit that environment. So PATH fix works. Good — this was the earlier bug (with %~dp0 unexpanded), now fixed with CURDIR.\n\n7. **Test for empty case redirects stderr to file**: `2> $(BINDIR)\\t3_bbox_cpp\\t3_empty_case` and stdout to nul. Then checks that the file contains the expected substring. Good — matches the agreed policy.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtle problem: the `.ok` test target for empty case writes stderr to a file inside `$(BINDIR)\\t3_bbox_cpp\\` which is also where main.exe lives. That's fine.\n\n8. **Exit code check for empty case**: NO_INPUT=66. The C++ program returns exit_code::no_in = 66. C program returns no_input = 66. Good.\n\n9. **Wait, potential issue: `t3_cpp_empty_case.ok` depends on `tests\\expect\\t3_empty_case` but the recipe redirects stderr into `$(BINDIR)\\t3_bbox_cpp\\t3_empty_case`. Then `$(FIND_SUBSTR) $(BINDIR)\\t3_bbox_cpp\\t3_empty_case tests\\expect\\t3_empty_case`. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtle thing: the empty input file `tests\\input_data\\t3_empty_case` is 0 bytes. `$(T3_CPP_EXE) < tests\\input_data\\t3_empty_case`. getline returns false immediately → points empty → prints \"Пустые данные отсутствуют\" to stderr, returns 66. Good.\n\n10. **Potential issue: `check_contains` arg order.** check_contains.cpp: `file1 file2`, checks if file2 content is substring of file1 content. Usage: `$(FIND_SUBSTR) <result> <expected>` — result is file1, expected is file2. Good — matches \"ищет подстроку file2 в file1\". In t2 tests: `$(FIND_SUBSTR) $(BINDIR)\\t2_passport_cpp\\t2_norm tests\\expect\\t2_norm` — result first, expected second. Good.\n\n11. **Missing: mass tests.** This is the main task-spec gap. §6.1 explicitly requires gen_cloud mass tests for t3 and t4. The Makefile builds gen_cloud but doesn't use it. Also the independent reference (t3_bbox_ref.ps1 or a separate exe) is not wired into tests. t3_bbox_ref.ps1 is gitignored (it's in .gitignore line 13). Interesting — so the reference is not committed. Per policy §4.9.3, etalons are generated on the fly by independent reference. The ps1 reference exists locally but is gitignored. So mass tests would need either the ps1 wired (but it's gitignored → clean clone wouldn't have it → make test would fail). This is a real issue worth flagging.\n\nHmm, but wait — the user said \"всё собирается и тесты зелёные\" and asks me to re-check \"всё для таск3\". So my job: review the t3 part of the Makefile carefully and report issues. I should flag the mass-test gap as the biggest one, plus dead code.\n\n12. **Check the `t3` phony target and `all`**: `all: t1 t2 t3`, `t3: $(T3_CPP_EXE) $(T3_C_EXE)`. Good. `test` includes T3 tests. Good.\n\n13. **`format`/`format-check` wildcard `**/*.cpp`** — in GNU make, `$(wildcard **/*.cpp)` — the `**` glob. GNU make's wildcard uses `*` which matches within one directory level... Actually `**/*.cpp` in make: `**` is not special in make (unlike shell globstar). make treats `**/*.cpp` — hmm, does make support `**`? In GNU make, wildcard expands `*` which does NOT cross directory boundaries. So `**/*.cpp` — the `**` matches... Actually in make's glob implementation (fnmatch-based), `*` matches any chars including `/`? Let me think. GNU make's wildcard function: \"Wildcard expansion is performed by make using the same algorithm as the shell\" but `*` does match across... Actually make's wildcard is directory-aware; `*/*.cpp` matches one level down. `**/*.cpp` — the first `**` matches a directory name (can't contain `/` since it's a single component). So `**/*.cpp` = `*/*.cpp` effectively (both `*` chars are within one path component). It matches files one level deep in any subdirectory. So `t3_bbox_cpp\\main.cpp`, `common\\geometry.cpp`, `gen_cloud\\main.cpp`, `tests\\run_case.cpp` — all one level deep. It works. Not a t3-specific issue anyway. Skip.\n\n14. **Encoding of Makefile itself**: comments are garbled in my read (CP1251 shown as garbage). The Makefile is in CP1251 (ANSI). That's consistent with repo policy for source files. clang-format doesn't touch .mk/Makefile. Fine.\n\nWait, actually — is the Makefile saved in CP1251? The read tool shows mojibake, meaning it's not UTF-8. Probably CP1251. That matches repo encoding policy. OK.\n\n15. **`t3_c_test1` etc. for C**: same expect files as C++ (t3_test1, t3_test2, t3_test3 shared). C and C++ programs must produce identical output. Both print \"Количество точек: ...\", etc. C uses printf with %.3f, C++ uses std::print {:.3f}. For these specific values they should match. Tests pass. But there's a subtle floating-point: C++ uses std::reduce and std::hypot; C uses manual accumulation and sqrt. For test3: center x = (0+0+16-16)/4 = 0 exactly, so 0.000 in both. avg dist = 16.000 exactly in both? hypot/sqrt of exact values. Test2: 0.333, 0.654 — the values must match between C and C++ to 3 decimals. Tests pass, so fine.\n\nBut — hmm, one thing worth noting: the expect files for t3_test1/2/3 are shared between C and C++ versions. If the C and C++ outputs differ in even one digit (e.g., 0.654 vs 0.654), they'd match. If differ (0.654 vs 0.653), C test would fail. Since green, OK.\n\n16. **Potential issue: `t3_cpp_test2` and `t3_cpp_test3` and `t3_cpp_test1`** — the test targets write stdout to `$(BINDIR)\\t3_bbox_cpp\\t3_test1` etc. These files are in the same dir as the exe. Fine. But they're not cleaned explicitly — `clean` removes build dir. Fine.\n\n17. **Line 364 comment**: \"make запускает каждую команду в новой cmd, поэтому set PATH не сохранится\" — wait, the comment says: \"make запускает каждую команду в новой cmd, поэтому set PATH сохранится тут, т.к. он нужен для всех следующих команд\". Actually the recipe uses `$(SET_DLL_PATH) && \\` at the start of each multi-line recipe, so within a single recipe invocation (one cmd), the set applies to the whole chain. That's the correct approach. Good.\n\nWait — actually there's a subtlety. Each test rule is a separate recipe line set? No — each rule has ONE recipe with multiple lines joined by `\\`. So it's one cmd invocation: `set PATH=... && run_case ... && find_substr ... && touch`. All in one cmd session. PATH applies. \n\nBut hold on: `run_case.exe` itself uses `std::system(argv[1])` which spawns ANOTHER cmd. Does the inner cmd inherit the PATH? Yes, environment is inherited. So main.exe launched by inner cmd finds the DLL. Good.\n\n18. **Real issue I should double-check: `$(SET_DLL_PATH)` is a `:=`? No, it's `:=`... wait line 365: `SET_DLL_PATH := set \"PATH=$(CURDIR)\\$(BINDIR)\\common;%PATH%\"`.** It's `:=` simple assignment. CURDIR is expanded at definition time — fine, CURDIR is set early by make. BINDIR expanded at definition time. Fine. The `%PATH%` stays literal (make doesn't expand `%`), passed to cmd which expands it. Good.\n\n19. **Missing: t3 tests don't check `--version`/CLI? Not relevant for t3.**\n\n20. **The `test` phony echo message says \"T1_CPP T1_C T2_CPP T2_C T3_CPP T3_C: ALL TESTS PASSED\"** — good.\n\n21. **Missing mass tests is the big one.** Also §6.1: \"Юнит-уровень (6a): ... геометрическое ядро (расстояние, центроид, вершина N-угольника)\" — unit tests for geometry core? For t1 there's no separate unit test exe in the Makefile either (t1 tests are acceptance-style via run_case + grep). Hmm, the §6.1 says unit-level: clean logic (vertex_word for t2, geometry core for t3). But in the Makefile, t1/t2 tests are all acceptance (run_case + findstr/check_contains). No separate unit test exes. This may be a broader gap not specific to t3. I'll mention but not overload.\n\nActually wait — let me reconsider scope. The user asked specifically: \"Makefile, проверь всё для таск3\". So focus on t3 part. But also flag cross-cutting issues discovered in the t3 portion.\n\nLet me verify the actual output of programs matches expect files — I could run the tests... but plan mode forbids modifications. Running make test would create build artifacts. Hmm, but the user says tests are green already. I shouldn't run them (that would modify files — building). In plan mode I must not. So I rely on static review.\n\nLet me also double check the t3_too_few_args_case expect: 51 bytes. \"Строка 1. Ожидалось координаты X Y Z. Получено: 2 3\" — let me count: Строка(6) space(1) 1(1) .(1) space(1) Ожидалось(9) space(1) координаты(10) space(1) X(1) space(1) Y(1) space(1) Z(1) .(1) space(1) Получено(8) :(1) space(1) 2(1) space(1) 3(1) = 6+1+1+1+1+9+1+10+1+1+1+1+1+1+1+8+1+1+1+1+1 = 51. Yes exactly 51, no trailing newline. Good.\n\nBut wait — C main.c parse_point error path for too few: `fprintf(stderr, \"Строка %d. Ожидалось координаты X Y Z. Получено: %s\\n\", i, s);` where s = \"2 3\" (the raw string). s would include trailing? get_string reads a line, \"2 3\" no newline at EOF... Actually the input file t3_too_few_args_case is \"2 3\" (3 bytes, no trailing newline). get_string in C reads until EOF. Hmm, earlier journal noted a bug fixed where get_string returned EOF error even with content. Now expect shows \"Получено: 2 3\". Fine.\n\nFor C++: main.cpp line 39: `std::cerr << \"Строка \" << i << \". Ожидалось координаты X Y Z. Получено: \" << temp << \"\\n\";` — temp is the getline string \"2 3\". OK.\n\nBut hold on, C++ prints a trailing \"\\n\" after \"2 3\" — result file has \"...Получено: 2 3\\n\" (or \\r\\n). Expect file has no trailing newline. Substring match still OK.\n\nNow the not_digit test: input \"3 2 bam\" (7 bytes). C++: prints \"Строка 1. Нечисловое данное: 3 2 bam\". Expect matches. Good.\n\nHmm wait, let me double-check C++ main.cpp message. Line 39-42:\n```\nif (result.error() == 1)\n    std::cerr << \"Строка \" << i << \". Ожидалось координаты X Y Z. Получено: \" << temp << \"\\n\";\nelse\n    std::cerr << \"Строка \" << i << \". Нечисловое данное: \" << temp << \"\\n\";\n```\nAnd C main.c:\n```\nif (ex_code == 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Строка %d. Ожидалось координаты X Y Z. Получено: %s\\n\", i, s);\n}\nif (ex_code == 2) {\n    fprintf(stderr, \"Строка %d. Нечисловое данное: %s\\n\", i, s);\n}\n```\nConsistent. Good.\n\nNow the empty input message: C++ main.cpp line 52: `std::cerr << \"Пустые данные отсутствуют\\n\";` C main.c line 104: `fprintf(stderr, \"Пустые данные отсутствуют\\n\");`. Expect: \"Пустые данные отсутствуют\" (26 bytes = 25 chars + ... wait 26 bytes). Let me count: Пустые(6) space(1) данные(6) space(1) отсутствуют(12) = 26. Yes. So expect = full message, no trailing newline. Good.\n\nNow let me reconsider the mass tests gap more concretely. §6.1 for t3: \"Массовый вход (задачи 3–4): генератор облака точек gen_cloud --size --seed (std::mt19937, фиксированный seed), размеры 10 / 100 / 10⁶; данные в build/ под .gitignore.\" and \"эталон — независимая референс-реализация на лету (для задачи 1 — формула...; для задач 3–4 — однострочник PowerShell или отдельный простой exe-референс)\".\n\nThe Makefile: gen_cloud.exe is built but never used. No mass tests. This is a genuine gap vs the task spec. The bat test (t3_build_and_test.bat) also didn't have mass tests — so this is a known incomplete area. I should flag it and ask whether it's in scope now.\n\nAlso: the ref script t3_bbox_ref.ps1 is in .gitignore — meaning it's NOT committed. Per §4.9.3, etalons computed by independent reference on the fly. For mass tests we'd need the ps1 available in the repo. Currently gitignored → clean clone lacks it. That's a conflict: if we wire ps1 into make test, a clean clone would fail because the ps1 isn't there. Need to decide: commit the ps1 (as the \"reference implementation\" source — §4.9.3 says \"В репозитории — только исходники (генератор, программа, референс)\" — the reference SOURCE should be in repo!). Actually §4.9.3: \"В репозитории — только исходники (генератор, программа, референс) и сборочные файлы. Эталон считает независимая референс-реализация\". So the reference (ps1) SHOULD be committed; the etalon OUTPUT is generated on the fly. But currently t3_bbox_ref.ps1 and t4_filter_ref.ps1 are gitignored. That contradicts the policy. Worth flagging.\n\nHmm, but wait — maybe the user deliberately gitignored refs (journal entry 2026-07-23: \"убрала референсы из удалённого репозитория через git rm --cached ... и добавила их в .gitignore\"). So it was a deliberate decision. But it conflicts with §4.9.3. Actually the policy says reference implementation source should be in repo. The gitignore decision seems to contradict the mentor's own materials. I should flag this as a question — maybe the user has a reason, or maybe it's an oversight. Per anti-sycophancy, I should raise it.\n\nActually, re-reading: journal 2026-07-23 says refs were removed from the remote repo and added to gitignore. That seems to be a deliberate act at the time. But §4.9.3 says references belong in the repo. There's tension. I'll raise it as a question.\n\nAlso: for the current literal tests, the etalons in tests\\expect\\t3_test1 etc. are committed snapshots. §4.9.3 says \"Замороженный снапшот выхода — не эталон корректности: он фиксирует и ошибку, если она была в первом прогоне.\" Hmm. For literal tests, the expected output is hand-verified (the values are mathematically checkable: 1 point → all equal, center = point, dist 0; test2: 0.333/0.654; test3: symmetric → 0.000/16.000). So these are legit hand-computed etalons, not blind snapshots. Fine. But the mass tests DO need on-the-fly reference.\n\nLet me also verify: are there any issues with the `.ok` test files and their dependencies (input files, expect files as prerequisites)? \n\nt3_cpp_test1.ok: `$(T3_CPP_EXE) $(RUN_CASE) $(FIND_SUBSTR) tests\\input_data\\t3_test1 tests\\expect\\t3_test1 | $(TESTDIR)` — good, depends on input and expect. If either changes, test re-runs. Good (this was a fix from the journal — \"я не учла, что .ok файлы должны зависеть от своих входных данных и эталона\"). Nice.\n\nBut the recipe output file `$(BINDIR)\\t3_bbox_cpp\\t3_test1` — not a prerequisite/order-only; fine since it's regenerated.\n\nOne more: empty case redirects stderr to `$(BINDIR)\\t3_bbox_cpp\\t3_empty_case` — but if that file already exists from a previous run and the test re-runs... it gets overwritten. Fine.\n\nNow potential BUG: **the empty-case and error-case tests write stderr output file, but the output files for empty case (`t3_empty_case`), etc. could be stale**. No — they're always rewritten by `2>`. Fine.\n\nLet me check the `TOUCH` mechanism: `type nul > $@` creates empty file. If recipe's chain before touch fails, `&&` short-circuits → touch not run → .ok not updated → make reports error. Good, that's the correct pattern.\n\nWait — but there's a subtle issue with `$(TOUCH) $@`: `TOUCH := type nul > `. So `type nul > $@`. On first run, `$@` doesn't exist → cmd creates it. On subsequent runs, if all deps unchanged, target exists & newer → not rebuilt. Good.\n\nNow — one concern: **`$(RUN_CASE)` uses `std::system` which returns the process exit code; on Windows `std::system` returns the wait status... Actually MSVC `system()` returns the exit code directly if it can. The run_case compares `real_code != expected_code`. For exit code 66, system returns 66. Works (t1/t2 already use it). Fine.\n\nNow let me think about whether the `SET_DLL_PATH` + `&&` works with cmd. `set \"PATH=...;%PATH%\" && command` — set is internal cmd, returns success. Works. And make runs recipe via `cmd /c \"set ... && ...\"`. Fine.\n\nHmm, but wait: there might be an issue with **`%PATH%` and make**: make doesn't expand `%`, passes literally. cmd expands `%PATH%`. But cmd's expansion of `%PATH%` happens when the line is parsed. Since the whole thing is one cmd invocation, PATH expands to the OLD value + new prefix. Good.\n\nNow — another potential issue: **make might