pendency! If geometry.hpp changes, the dll isn't rebuilt → stale dll → main.exe uses old code. This is a real correctness gap in the build graph. Similarly string_utils_cpp_dll.obj (line 191) missing string_utils.hpp. And t3 main.obj missing header deps.\n\nThis is worth flagging — though it's a known manual-deps limitation at this learning stage (layer 15 notes). But the dll objs at least list .cpp; they should list .hpp too. t1/t2's static objs DO list headers (lines 172-182 include .hpp/.h). So the dynamic objs are inconsistent — they forgot the headers. Flag it.\n\nAlso t3 main.obj: no header deps. Same as t1/t2 main.obj pattern (they also don't list headers). So main.obj header-deps missing across the board — but the pattern rule can't easily include headers. This is the documented MSVC manual-deps limitation. I'll mention it briefly as known/acceptable but note the dll objs inconsistency (static objs list headers, dll objs don't).\n\nLet me also check the t1/t2 for consistency with the review request — no, focus t3.\n\nNow — let me reconsider: is there anything actually WRONG that would cause false-green? Let me think about test weakness:\n- The norm tests (t3_test1/2/3) check substring of FULL expected output. If the program printed extra garbage lines but still contained the expected substring, the test would pass. But check_contains with the whole 4-line block as substring — it checks the entire block appears. If program prints the same 4 lines, fine. If program prints extra wrong stuff in between? Would the 4 correct lines still appear contiguously? Possibly. Weakness: substring, not full equality. But same approach as t2_norm (accepted). OK.\n\n- Actually wait, there IS a subtle bug potential: the norm test redirects stdout to `$(BINDIR)\\t3_bbox_cpp\\t3_test1` and checks contains. But it does NOT redirect stderr to nul for the norm tests! Let me check line 385: `$(RUN_CASE) \"$(T3_CPP_EXE) < tests\\input_data\\t3_test1 > $(BINDIR)\\t3_bbox_cpp\\t3_test1 2> $(NULLDEV)\" 0` — yes it does `2> $(NULLDEV)`. Good.\n\n- Error-case tests: `> $(NULLDEV) 2> file` — stdout to nul, stderr to file, exit code checked, then stderr file checked. Good, matches agreed policy.\n\nNow, the exit code checks: run_case checks `real_code != expected_code`. For too_few args the expected is DATA=65. Program returns 65. Good. For not_digit DATA=65. Good.\n\nAlright, let me also verify there are no issues with `.PHONY: all t1 t2 t3 test ...` — includes t3. Good.\n\nAlso `all: t1 t2 t3` — good.\n\nNow the missing pieces summary:\n1. **Mass tests with gen_cloud (sizes 10/100/10⁶) + independent reference not in Makefile.** gen_cloud.exe is defined+built but unused → dead in graph.\n2. **t3_bbox_ref.ps1 gitignored** — conflicts with §4.9.3 (reference source should be in repo). If we add mass tests wired to ps1, clean clone breaks.\n3. **Dead code:** lines 58-59 (COMMON_CPP_DLL_OBJ, COMMON_C_DLL_OBJ unused duplicates of CPP_DLL_OBJ/C_DLL_OBJ), line 71 commented T3_CPP_OBJ.\n4. **Dll obj rules missing header deps** (geometry.hpp, string_utils.hpp) — inconsistent with static obj rules which list them.\n5. Possible: **CURDIR-based PATH** fine.\n\nAlso, should double check the `t3` target echo. Fine.\n\nLet me also reconsider: the grouped target rule line 216: `$(BINDIR)\\common\\common_cpp.dll $(BINDIR)\\common\\common_cpp.lib &: $(CPP_DLL_OBJ) | $(BINDIR)\\common` with recipe `$(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) /DLL /OUT:$(BINDIR)\\common\\common_cpp.dll $^`. Note: `$^` here = both objs. Good. /OUT hardcoded to dll — correct (per our discussion: $@ in grouped recipe would be the triggering target, could be lib → wrong). Good, she followed the advice.\n\nBut wait — one thing: with `&:`, if the `.lib` is removed but `.dll` is present, does make rebuild? Yes: \"if any of the grouped targets are out of date, all the grouped targets are considered out of date\". The user asked about this earlier — deleting lib triggers rebuild of the group. Good. And main.exe depends