onsumes), not dll! Since lib and dll are grouped, depending on lib will rebuild both. So the FIX: make main.exe depend on `.lib` (and the runtime needs dll via PATH). Actually ideally prereq = the lib (import lib). The dll is needed at runtime, handled via PATH set in tests. So change prereq from common_cpp.dll to common_cpp.lib. Then: if lib deleted → group out of date → rebuild → both exist. \n\nThis is a real, concrete improvement. Currently main.exe's prereq on dll is slightly wrong semantically (link consumes lib, not dll). Since grouped targets make lib a real node, the natural edge is lib. I'll flag this.\n\nWait, but careful: does the runtime PATH setup need dll as prereq? The dll is produced by the same grouped rule as lib. If main.exe depends on lib, and lib is rebuilt, dll is rebuilt too (grouped). So dll will exist. And tests set PATH to common dir. Good. So depend on lib, not dll.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: what if dll deleted but lib fresh? Group out of date (dll missing) → but is the group needed? main.exe depends on lib (fresh) → not rebuilt → runtime: main.exe runs but dll missing → 0xC0000135. Edge case: someone manually deletes dll. With main.exe depending on lib only, dll deletion wouldn't trigger rebuild. Depending on BOTH dll and lib would cover both. Actually with grouped targets: if main.exe's prereqs are both dll and lib (both members of group), then when either is missing → group rule fires → both rebuilt. That's the most robust: list both as prereqs. Since they're one group, make handles \"any out of date → rebuild all\". Let me suggest: prereq `$(BINDIR)\\common\\common_cpp.dll $(BINDIR)\\common\\common_cpp.lib` (both), recipe `$< $(BINDIR)\\common\\common_cpp.lib`. Hmm, but $< would be main.obj (first prereq) — order matters. Currently `$<` is main.obj, works. If we add lib as second prereq, `$<` still main.obj. Good.\n\nActually, simpler and correct: since lib and dll are grouped, listing either one as prereq makes the group rebuild when that one is missing. To cover \"dll deleted\" too, list both. I'll recommend both (or just lib if she decides dll deletion is not a realistic manual scenario). I'll present the tradeoff and let her decide.\n\nHmm wait, but actually, if only lib is prereq and someone deletes only dll: at runtime tests would fail with 0xC0000135. But that's an unusual manual action. The realistic scenario is clean build or source change. I'll recommend depending on the lib (the actual link input) and note the dll-deletion edge case; or list both for robustness. Let her choose.\n\nActually — thinking more: simplest robust: `main.exe: main.obj common_cpp.dll common_cpp.lib`. Both grouped members as prereqs. Then any missing → rebuild. And recipe links with `$(BINDIR)\\common\\common_cpp.lib` explicitly. `$<` = main.obj. Fine.\n\nOK, I have a solid review. Let me also quickly check the tools build rule line 236-242 for check_contains: `$(CXX) /Fo:$(BINDIR)\\tools\\check_contains.obj /Fe:$@ $(CXXFLAGS) $<`. Fine.\n\nNow let me structure the review. The user is in plan mode; I should present findings as a plan (issues to fix). Since I'm read-only, I present the review and ask whether to fix. Let me organize by severity:\n\n**A. Пропущенные массовые тесты (главный пробел против §6.1):** gen_cloud built but unused; no size 10/100/10⁶ tests; no independent reference wired. Also §4.9.3 — reference should be in repo, but ps1 gitignored.\n\n**B. Граф сборки main.exe:** prereq is dll, but link consumes lib. With grouped targets, better to depend on lib (both). Covers deleted-lib case.\n\n**C. Мёртвый код:** lines 58-59 unused duplicates; line 71 commented leftover.\n\n**D. Header-зависимости dll obj:** missing geometry.hpp/string_utils.hpp in dll obj rules (inconsistent with static obj rules).\n\nLet me also double check something about the `t3` test names and the `.gitignore` for generated output files — build/ ignored, *.txt ignored. The generated test output goes to build/... fine.\n\nAlso — check `.gitignore`: `*.txt` is ignored (line 8). But the expect/input_data files have no extension — fine, not ignored. build/ ignored. gen_cloud outputs to stdout (not files). OK.\n\nOne more: **the t3 test recipes use `$(SET_DLL_PATH)` global var** — defined at line 365 in the middle of the file. Global var defined before use is fine in make (definition order doesn't matter for := at parse... actually := is immediate; defined at line 365, used after → fine).\n\nLet me also double check `t3_cpp_empty_case.ok` depends on `$(T3_CPP_EXE) $(RUN_CASE) $(FIND_SUBSTR)` AND input/expect — yes. Good.\n\nAlso — **shared expect files between C and C++**: if C and C++ outputs differ slightly (e.g., C printf %.3f vs C++ std::print {:.3f} rounding), one of them would fail. Both pass currently. But this coupling means changing one program's message breaks the other's test — acceptable, arguably good (forces consistency). Not a bug.\n\nLet me also sanity check: does the C version print \"Строка 1.\" with same wording? C main.c line 62: `\"Строка %d. Ожидалось координаты X Y Z. Получено: %s\\n\"`. C++ line 39: `\"Строка \" << i << \". Ожидалось координаты X Y Z. Получено: \"`. Both same. Good.\n\nWait, I should double-check the C++ main.cpp more carefully — earlier read showed line 39: `std::cerr << \"Строка \" << i << \". Ожидалось координаты X Y Z. Получено: \" << temp << \"\\n\";` — but that's from the CP1251-garbled read. The expect file hex shows \"Ожидалось координаты X Y Z. Получено: 2 3\". Consistent with both. Good.\n\nNow, one more consideration — the `format-check` in `test` prereq. `test: format-check $(T1...)` — format-check is phony, so every `make test` runs clang-format --dry-run first. Fine, that's intended policy.\n\nLet me also verify: is there a rule that gen_cloud should also be formatted? `format:` wildcard includes gen_cloud\\main.cpp (it's `**/*.cpp`). Good.\n\nI think I have enough. Let me also quickly verify there are no other t3 references I missed 